// REJECTED — A5: a crossing of a computer-use verb whose registry class has
// NO INVERSE, whose extern nevertheless declares a `compensate`. This is A5's
// second half, and it is the half that keeps the rule from being read as
// "declare a compensate whenever you cross": a verb the registry says is
// `unknown` — `ui.click` acts on the user's screen and nothing in the family
// can undo the click itself — must NOT claim a compensation, because a
// compensation that cannot restore the state it names is worse than none.
//
// Without this half the rule would be satisfiable by adding a `compensate` to
// every extern, which is the direction the row's `witness_bites` theorem
// exercises. The refusal is again read off the checker's REPORTED CODE — G4
// with category `reversibility` — not off the filename.
//
// expected error:
//   a5_no_inverse_declares_compensate.rvl:23: `emission[ui.click]` is
//     unknown, so extern `actuate` may not declare `compensate`
//     remove the `compensate` clause, or cross a verb the registry lists as
//     compensatable

extern pure fn clear_amount()
  = @py { return None }

extern emission[ui.click] fn actuate(s: Str) compensate clear_amount()
  = @py { return None }
